Create a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to write a
Python HTML minifier script with the following specification: Write a Python 3
script (intended to run under Termux on Android, with a
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python` shebang) that minifies HTML
files.
Use `from __future__ import annotations`, and import the standard `re`, `sys`,
and `pathlib.Path`, plus `typing.TYPE_CHECKING`.
Import `get_files` and `mpf` from a local helper module named `dh`, and use
`lxml.html` for DOM parsing.
Import `HtmlElement` from `lxml.html` only under `TYPE_CHECKING`.
Core design: implement a class `HTMLMinifier` whose constructor accepts these
boolean keyword options with defaults: `remove_comments=True`,
`collapse_whitespace=True`, `remove_empty_attributes=True`,
`remove_optional_tags=False`, `minify_css=False`, `minify_js=False`.
Store each as an attribute, and store a private set of tags to preserve
whitespace in: `pre`, `textarea`, `code`, `script`, `style`.
The public method `minify(html_str: str) -> str` should parse the input with
`lxml.html.fromstring`; if that raises `lxml.html.ParserError`, fall back to
`lxml.html.fragment_fromstring` with `create_parent=True`.
Then run a recursive node-processing pass (`_process_node`) that respects the
preserve set and applies the enabled options (comment removal, whitespace
collapsing outside preserved tags, removal of empty attributes, optional tag
removal, and optional CSS/JS minification).
Serialize with `lxml.html.tostring(..., encoding="unicode", method="html")` and
pass the result through a `_post_process` step that performs final regex-based
cleanup (e.g., trimming whitespace between tags and tidying leftover spacing).
Command-line behavior: the script should be runnable directly.
It should use `get_files` from `dh` to resolve input files (accepting paths or
patterns from `sys.argv`), read each file, minify it with an `HTMLMinifier`
instance, and write the result.
Use `pathlib.Path` for all file handling.
Handle errors per file so one failing file does not stop processing of the rest;
report failures to stderr and exit with a nonzero status if any file failed.
Report successes or summary counts to stdout.
If no arguments or no matching files are given, print a usage message to stderr
and exit nonzero.
Use `mpf` from `dh` where the original design calls for it (multi-file
processing or progress output); treat it as an external helper and do not
reimplement it.
Edge cases to handle: empty input, input that is not valid HTML (fragment
fallback), content inside preserved tags left untouched, unreadable or non-UTF-8
files (report and continue), and output paths that cannot be written.
Write the complete script as a single file, with a `main()` function guarded by
`if __name__ == "__main__":`, and keep the code style clean with type hints
throughout. ---
